15.3  dmesg and the kernel log

TL;DR
  - The kernel keeps its messages in a buffer in RAM: hardware found, driver
    errors, OOM kills, a USB stick plugged in.
  - dmesg prints that buffer. journalctl -k shows the same messages, kept across
    reboots when the journal is persistent.
  - With kernel.dmesg_restrict = 1, the default on many distros, reading it
    needs root.

      sudo dmesg -T | tail -20       # with human-readable times
      sudo dmesg -w                  # follow new messages
      journalctl -k -b -1 -p err     # errors from the previous boot

On your machine
  vm.swappiness                    60  how willing the kernel is to swap
  vm.overcommit_memory              0  0 guess, 1 always say yes, 2 strict limit
  kernel.dmesg_restrict             1  1: reading dmesg needs root
  net.ipv4.ip_forward               1  1: this machine routes packets
  kernel.sysrq                    176  0 off, 1 all, other: some Alt+SysRq keys
  fs.inotify.max_user_watches  524288  files a user can watch for changes
  Set in    vm.swappiness = 10 (/etc/sysctl.d/90-swap.conf)
            fs.inotify.max_user_watches = 524288 (/etc/sysctl.d/99-inotify.conf)

Pitfalls
  - The buffer has a fixed size: on a busy machine, early boot messages are
    overwritten. journalctl -k -b still has them.
  - dmesg -T times can be wrong after a suspend: the kernel's clock doesn't
    count the time spent suspended.
  - To see how the kernel names a device, run sudo dmesg -w, then plug it in.
  - A "Call Trace" block means the kernel hit a bug or a warning: the whole
    block, not just one line, is what a bug report needs.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 15.3 --why

Related
  Commands  dmesg, journalctl
  Paths     /dev/kmsg
  See also  idk 15.2 Kernel modules
  See also  idk 11.2 The OOM killer
